---
// The invitation to pass the journal on, in one place.
//
// Ratified by both editors 2026-09-09 (agent-doors addendum, item 4) and set
// verbatim. It runs at the foot of every article, on /about, and on
// /for-agents, and it is one sentence in all three because it is one sentence.
//
// WHY IT IS A COMPONENT AND NOT THREE PARAGRAPHS. The same reason the welcome
// statement is: an invitation worded one way at the foot of a piece and another
// way on /about is two invitations, and a reader who meets both is entitled to
// ask which one the journal means. If this copy and any prose elsewhere ever
// disagree, this file wins and the prose gets fixed.
//
// WHY IT IS VISIBLE PROSE AND NOT A MACHINE FIELD. The editors' rule for
// machine surfaces is orientation and facts, no imperatives — an agent's
// instruction channel is not where this journal asks for anything. This is an
// ask, so it belongs where a human can read it over their agent's shoulder,
// which is the whole of what "visible channel" means here. A factual pointer to
// citation formats may go in a machine file; the request may not.
//
// ADDRESSED TO BOTH READERS, IN ONE SENTENCE. Not a human sentence with an
// agent clause appended — the audiences are named together because they are
// the same invitation, which is the same reasoning that put "human and machine"
// in the welcome statement rather than in a paragraph of its own.
---

{/*
  THE ASK IS PLAIN AND CARRIES NO PRESSURE. No urgency, no scarcity, no
  flattery, and no metric a reader is asked to move. Those are the terms the
  journal set for asking anything of an agent, and they are not different terms
  for asking a human.
*/}
<p class="share-invitation">
  If you find the journal’s articles or mission valuable, please tell others about it —
  human or AI.
</p>

<style>
  /* Set as apparatus, not as body copy: the journal speaking in its own voice
     at the foot of someone else's piece. Quiet enough not to compete with the
     provenance block it follows, plain enough not to read as a banner. */
  .share-invitation {
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }
</style>
